  "content": {
    "id": "p8-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "Suppose that $a\\geq0$ and $b\\geq0$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p8-q7-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Is it generally true that $\\sqrt{a+b}=\\sqrt{a}+\\sqrt{b}$?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No. For $a=b=1$, the two sides are $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$.",
          "worked": "No. Take $a=b=1$. Then $\\sqrt{a+b}=\\sqrt{2}$, whereas $\\sqrt{a}+\\sqrt{b}=2$. Since $\\sqrt{2}\\ne2$, the identity is not generally true.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      },
      {
        "id": "p8-q7-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Determine for which $a$ and $b$ we have $\\sqrt{a+b}=\\sqrt{a}+\\sqrt{b}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a=0$ or $b=0$.\n\nSquaring the equality gives $2\\sqrt{a}\\sqrt{b}=0$.",
          "worked": "Both sides are nonnegative, so squaring preserves equivalence. The required equality holds exactly when\n$$\\begin{align*}(\\sqrt{a}+\\sqrt{b})^2&=a+b\\\\a+b+2\\sqrt{a}\\sqrt{b}&=a+b\\\\2\\sqrt{a}\\sqrt{b}&=0\\end{align*}$$This occurs exactly when $a=0$ or $b=0$. Either condition also makes the original equality true.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p8-q7-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Prove that $\\sqrt{a+b}\\leq\\sqrt{a}+\\sqrt{b}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\sqrt{a+b}\\leq\\sqrt{a}+\\sqrt{b}$.\n\nThe square of the right side is $a+b+2\\sqrt{a}\\sqrt{b}\\geq a+b$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}(\\sqrt{a}+\\sqrt{b})^2&=a+b+2\\sqrt{a}\\sqrt{b}\\\\(\\sqrt{a}+\\sqrt{b})^2&\\geq a+b\\end{align*}$$The cross term is nonnegative because $a,b\\geq0$. Both sides of the required inequality are nonnegative, so taking square roots gives $\\sqrt{a+b}\\leq\\sqrt{a}+\\sqrt{b}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      }
    ]
  },
